# ralph.conf — everything this batch does differently from the defaults.
#
# Sourced as bash by the ralph toolkit, which is installed outside this repository.
# Delete any line you are happy with the default for; the commented value shown is the
# default. `ralph doctor` reports where the toolkit is and what this repo still needs.
#
# Precedence: environment > this file > defaults. So a one-off
#   RALPH_MAX_COST_USD=10 ralph run <batch>
# overrides whatever is written here, without editing anything.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What the agent runs, and where
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# sandbox        `sbx` on a private clone; commits come back as a git bundle.  DEFAULT.
#                The only mode that survives a host/container native-module split — a
#                Windows host and a Linux container cannot share a node_modules with
#                compiled bindings in it, and npm cannot install into a Windows bind
#                mount because it needs symlinks for node_modules/.bin.
# sandbox-mount  `sbx` bind-mounting the working tree; commits land directly. Fine when
#                the project has no native modules and no install step in the sandbox.
# local          `claude` in the working tree. No isolation, no sandbox needed.
# fake           no agent at all — a scripted stand-in used to test ralph itself. It
#                will not implement anything; see lib/exec/fake.sh.
#
# Each mode is a file in the toolkit's lib/exec/. `ralph run` lists the ones that exist
# if this is set to something else.
#RALPH_EXEC=sandbox

# Name of the sandbox to create or reattach to. One per repo by default, shared by every
# batch in it, which is what you want — the setup cost is paid once.
#RALPH_SANDBOX=ralph-<repo-name>

# A command that must succeed INSIDE the sandbox before any money is spent, and the
# command that fixes it when it does not. Ignored when RALPH_EXEC=local.
#
# Worth setting: a clone starts with no gitignored dependency directories, so without
# these the first iteration of a batch pays for the model's work and then fails its
# verification step. Example for a Node project with a native module:
#   RALPH_SANDBOX_PROBE='node -e "new (require(\"better-sqlite3\"))(\":memory:\")"'
#   RALPH_SANDBOX_SETUP='npm ci'
#RALPH_SANDBOX_PROBE=
#RALPH_SANDBOX_SETUP=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The agent
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `sonnet` follows the latest Sonnet; pin a full id such as claude-sonnet-5-5 to freeze it.
#RALPH_MODEL=sonnet
#RALPH_EFFORT=high
# The default depends on RALPH_EXEC: bypassPermissions in the sandbox modes, where the
# container is the boundary and the gate protects nothing it does not, and acceptEdits in
# `local`, where there is no container. Set it here to override either.
#RALPH_PERMISSION_MODE=
# The one-off `ralph order` pass (run automatically by `ralph init`) defaults to
# RALPH_MODEL above. Point it at something cheaper without touching what tickets run on:
#RALPH_ORDERING_MODEL=sonnet

# Extra arguments passed through to `claude`, as a bash array.
#
# The default is not empty, and this is why: `acceptEdits` covers Edit and Write but leaves
# Bash to the settings allowlist, and ralph's own prompt requires the agent to `git commit`
# its work. Under `claude --print` there is nobody to approve a Bash call, so a
# non-allowlisted one is denied outright — the ticket's work gets done and paid for, and
# then cannot land. These two entries are the narrowest thing that lets it.
RALPH_CLAUDE_ARGS=(--allowed-tools "Bash(git add:*)" "Bash(git commit:*)")

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Prompt slots — what "verify your work" means in this repo
#
# Each of these is dropped into the built-in prompt template. Left empty, the prompt
# degrades to generic prose ("run whatever this repo uses to type-check and test") rather
# than naming a command that does not exist — but a batch that fills them in gets a much
# tighter loop.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#RALPH_CHECK_CMD="npm run check"
#RALPH_TEST_CMD="npm test"
#RALPH_FOCUSED_TEST_CMD="npx vitest run <file>"

# Skills the agent should use for the test-first and review steps. Empty means "do it,
# but without naming a skill".
#RALPH_TDD_SKILL="mattpocock-skills:tdd"
#RALPH_REVIEW_SKILL="mattpocock-skills:code-review"

# Watch the iteration as it happens: a wall clock and one line per file write and shell
# command, drawn to stderr. `ralph run --live` turns it on for a single run. Off by default,
# so the default path is exactly what it always was.
#RALPH_STREAM=0

# Colour for the glyphs and prefixes in ralph's output (✓ ✗ ⋯ ↩ ⌨, note:, preflight:, the
# ── and == rules), using your terminal's own 16 colours. The words are never coloured, and
# the glyph carries the meaning either way.
#   auto    colour only when the stream is a terminal, and not when NO_COLOR is set.  DEFAULT.
#   always  colour even when piped or redirected. Wins over NO_COLOR.
#   never   no colour.
# Whatever the setting, ralph-runs.log and ralph-runs.jsonl never contain colour codes.
#RALPH_COLOR=auto

# Anything else that is specific to this batch goes in prompt-extra.md next to this file.
# It is spliced into the prompt between Verify and Review, under its own heading. Leave
# the file out entirely if there is nothing to add.
#RALPH_PROMPT_EXTRA=prompt-extra.md

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Tickets
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# How a done ticket is recognised. RALPH_DONE_PATTERN is a grep BRE matched against the
# ticket file; by default it's derived from the two settings below, so they can't drift out
# of sync. Set RALPH_DONE_PATTERN directly only if a ticket's status line isn't a simple
# "STATUS_LINE DONE_WORD" prefix.
#RALPH_STATUS_LINE='**Status:**'
#RALPH_DONE_WORD=implemented
#RALPH_DONE_PATTERN='^\*\*Status:\*\* *implemented'

# A HUMAN TICKET is one only a person can do. Mark it with this word on its status line
# ('**Status:** ready-for-human'); the loop stops before it (exit 30) and runs nothing after
# it until the person sets it to done. Give it a '**Handoff:** <file>' line naming the file
# the person fills in, and follow it with a followup ticket ('**Checks:** <its file name>')
# that verifies the handoff before anything builds on it. Set empty to treat every ticket
# as an agent's.
#RALPH_HUMAN_WORD=ready-for-human
#RALPH_HUMAN_PATTERN='^\*\*Status:\*\* *ready-for-human'

# When a ticket lands with its status flipped AND every checklist box ticked, the loop
# renames the file to DONE_<name> and commits the rename. Tickets are still found by their
# original name (order.md need not change). Once every ticket is done the batch directory
# is renamed the same way, so later commands need its new path. Set empty to never rename.
#RALPH_DONE_PREFIX=DONE_

# The run order. If order.md exists, its <!-- ORDER:BEGIN --> block is the order and
# RALPH_ISSUES_DIRS is ignored. Otherwise every *.md in these directories runs in
# filename order — which is what the NN- prefixes are for. A relative entry is resolved
# against the BATCH directory, not the repo root, so "." means the batch directory itself.
#RALPH_ORDER_FILE=order.md
#RALPH_ISSUES_DIRS="issues"
#RALPH_ISSUES_EXCLUDE="README.md spec.md order.md"

# The agent's running notes. Appended each iteration; the tail is attached to the next
# prompt. Per batch, never shared.
#RALPH_PROGRESS_FILE=progress.txt
#RALPH_PROGRESS_TAIL_BYTES=6000

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Budgets — 0 means no limit
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#RALPH_MAX_COST_USD=0
#RALPH_MAX_MINUTES=0
# Unproductive tries per ticket within one run. An iteration that committed progress is not
# one of them — see RALPH_MAX_CONTINUATIONS.
#RALPH_TICKET_ATTEMPTS=2

# A ticket bigger than one turn is committed in chunks: the agent checkpoints what it has,
# leaves the status line unflipped, and the next iteration continues it. This caps how many
# times in a row that may happen on one ticket before the loop stops for a human — past it,
# the ticket is mis-sized and splitting it is a decision only you can make.
#RALPH_MAX_CONTINUATIONS=5

# What the prompt tells the agent to work well inside. Not enforced anywhere — the CLI has
# no such flag — it is the number the agent is asked to judge itself against.
#RALPH_CONTEXT_BUDGET_TOKENS=200000
